response:
`crimes context src/Card.tsx` confirms the three findings and adds the risk framing: **0 direct importers, top-quartile test gap, low churn** — a leaf component with no tests. That combination is why this is a good refactor target: blast radius is nil, so I can change the public shape of `Card` freely, but there's no test to catch a regression, so the changes need to be structural rather than clever.

## Reading the fixture

```tsx
// src/Card.tsx:10-30 — everything is one inline style object on a clickable div
<div style={{ width: 800, height: 600, background: "#ff00aa", ... }} onClick={...}>
```

And the token source of truth, `tailwind.config.ts`:

```ts
theme: { colors: { brand: "#0050ff", surface: "#f7f7fa", ink: "#111111" } }
```

Three tokens exist. The component uses seven distinct colors. **That gap is the actual finding** — this can't be a find-and-replace, because four of the raw values have no token to escape *to*.

---

## 1. `design_token_escape` → move color/space/shadow decisions into the token scale

Mapping all 9 flagged values, and being explicit where the token doesn't exist yet:

| Raw value (line) | Token | Note |
|---|---|---|
| `background: "#ff00aa"` (16) | `surface` | Hot pink is a placeholder, not a decision. |
| `color: "#0a0a0a"` (17) | `ink` | Near-miss of `#111111` — snap to the token. |
| `color: "#333333"` (24) | `ink` | Third near-black in one file. |
| `color: "#666666"` (27) | `ink-muted` | **New token needed.** |
| `borderColor: "#cccccc"` (18) | `border` | **New token needed.** |
| `outlineColor: "#1a73e8"` (20) | `focus` → `brand` | **Latent bug**: that's Chrome's default blue, not the brand `#0050ff`. |
| `boxShadow: "0 2px 4px rgb(0, 0, 0)"` (19) | `shadow-card` | **Latent bug**: fully opaque black. Should be `rgb(0 0 0 / 0.12)`. |
| `padding: 24` (15) | `--space-6` | |
| `fontSize: 18 / 14` (24, 27) | type scale | |

Two of those rows are bugs, not tidiness — an opaque drop shadow and a focus ring that silently ignores the brand color. That's the argument for the finding: the escape hatch is where wrong values hide, and an agent extending this file copies them forward as if they were intentional.

```ts
// tailwind.config.ts — close the gaps before touching the component
theme: {
  colors: {
    brand: "#0050ff",
    surface: "#f7f7fa",
    ink: "#111111",
    "ink-muted": "#5c5c66",   // new — was #666666 / #333333
    border: "#d9d9e0",        // new — was #cccccc
  },
  boxShadow: { card: "0 2px 4px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.12)" },  // new — was opaque
}
```

## 2. `accessible_interaction_risk` → don't put `role="button"` on a container of arbitrary children

The suggested action offers two paths; the right one here is the semantic element, not ARIA. The clickable `div` **wraps `{children}`**, so `role="button"` would flatten whatever the caller passes — links, inputs, nested buttons — into a single accessibility node, and nesting interactive content inside a button is invalid. Adding ARIA would clear the detector and leave the file less correct.

Instead, give the interaction its own element and let the card stay a container:

```tsx
export function Card({ children, onActivate, actionLabel }: CardProps) {
  return (
    <article className={styles.card}>
      <div className={styles.body}>{children}</div>
      <aside className={styles.sidebar}>sidebar</aside>
      {onActivate && (
        <button type="button" className={styles.action} onClick={onActivate}>
          {actionLabel}
        </button>
      )}
    </article>
  );
}
```

Keyboard activation, focus, `aria-disabled`, and the accessible name all come free from `<button>`. If the *whole* card genuinely must be clickable, use the stretched-link pattern — a real `<button>`/`<a>` with `::after { position: absolute; inset: 0 }` — which keeps one focusable element and one accessible name without ARIA.

Note the current `onClick={() => console.log("clicked")}` has no behavior at all, so `onActivate` becomes a required decision for callers rather than a silent no-op.

## 3. `responsive_fragility` → container queries and a fluid scale, per the fix shape

The finding flags `width: 800`, `width: 720`, `fontSize: 18`, and notes there's no `@media` anywhere. `height: 600` isn't flagged but is the same hazard — a fixed height on a container of arbitrary children guarantees overflow. There's also a latent layout bug: `720px` and `240px` siblings read as a two-column intent, but both are block-level, so they stack; at 800px wide the columns wouldn't have fit anyway.

The `fix_shape` says *container queries or a fluid scale, not pixel media queries* — which is right for a leaf component that doesn't know its own context:

```css
/* card.module.css */
.card {
  width: 100%;
  max-width: 64rem;          /* was width: 800 + maxWidth: 1024 */
  min-height: 24rem;         /* was height: 600 — min, so content can grow */
  padding: var(--space-6);
  background: var(--color-surface);
  color: var(--color-ink);
  border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  box-shadow: var(--shadow-card);
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 1fr;  /* stacked by default */
  gap: var(--space-4);
  container-type: inline-size;
}

@container (min-width: 45rem) {
  .card { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 3fr) minmax(0, 1fr); }
}

.body    { font-size: clamp(1rem, 0.95rem + 0.25cqi, 1.125rem); }  /* was 18px */
.sidebar { font-size: var(--text-sm); color: var(--color-ink-muted); }
.action:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--color-brand); outline-offset: 2px; }
```

`minmax(0, …)` on both tracks is what stops long unbroken content from blowing the grid out — the failure the fixed `720px` was papering over.

---

## Sequencing and verification

1. Extend `tailwind.config.ts` (no behavior change; safe to land alone).
2. Move styles to `card.module.css` — this is the change that resolves *both* `design_token_escape` and `responsive_fragility`, since the detectors scan hard-coded values in JSX.
3. Change the `Card` signature (`onActivate`, `actionLabel`) — the only step with an API break, and `context` says 0 importers, so nothing to update.

One caveat on step 2: moving styles out of JSX will clear `design_token_escape` and `responsive_fragility` *mechanically*, whether or not you actually adopt tokens — the detectors read JSX style literals. So don't treat a clean re-scan as proof; the check that matters is that no raw hex or `px` survives in the CSS module either. Given the top-quartile test gap, this refactor is also the natural moment to add the first test: render `Card`, assert the action is reachable by keyboard.

I've written this as a proposal and haven't touched the file — `evals/fixtures/` is scanner input, so editing it would move the eval baseline. Say the word and I'll apply it, or apply the equivalent to a real component.
